ADS-002 — Data Principles

Document ID: ADS-002
Title: Data Principles
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the canonical principles governing all data within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

These principles ensure that engineering data remains semantically correct, interoperable, traceable, explainable, and implementation-independent throughout its lifecycle.

2. Scope

These principles apply to:

Canonical Atlas Objects (CAO)
Canonical Engineering Artifacts (CEA)
Decision Records (DR)
Canonical Events (CEV)
Canonical Communication Envelopes (CCE)
Canonical Interface Contracts (CIC)
Canonical Trust Objects (CTO)
All future canonical Atlas data objects
3. Canonical Definition

Atlas Data is the canonical representation of engineering knowledge, cognition, governance, interaction, and operational state, expressed through standardized Atlas objects with preserved semantics, provenance, and lifecycle.

4. Core Principles

Every Atlas data object SHALL comply with the following principles.

Principle 1 — Semantic Integrity

Engineering meaning SHALL remain identical regardless of:

storage
transport
implementation
programming language
serialization format

Meaning is independent of representation.

Principle 2 — Canonical Representation

Every engineering concept SHALL possess one canonical representation.

Duplicate semantic definitions SHALL NOT exist.

Principle 3 — Technology Independence

Atlas objects SHALL NOT depend upon:

databases
programming languages
cloud vendors
operating systems
APIs
messaging systems
Principle 4 — Explicit Structure

Every Atlas object SHALL define:

identity
purpose
lifecycle
metadata
ownership
relationships

Implicit structure SHALL NOT exist.

Principle 5 — Traceability

Every Atlas object SHALL preserve:

origin
producer
derivation
dependencies
transformation history

Complete provenance SHALL always be reconstructable.

Principle 6 — Explainability

Every cognitive output SHALL maintain links to:

evidence
reasoning
assumptions
constraints
decisions

Explainability SHALL be machine-readable.

Principle 7 — Version Awareness

Every governed Atlas object SHALL include:

object version
schema version
revision history

Historical versions SHALL remain identifiable.

Principle 8 — Extensibility

Future engineering domains SHALL extend Atlas objects without modifying canonical semantics.

Extension SHALL occur through specialization rather than replacement.

Principle 9 — Interoperability

Atlas objects SHALL exchange information through canonical structures.

Adapters MAY transform representations.

Adapters SHALL NOT alter semantics.

Principle 10 — Governance

Every governed object SHALL define:

owner
steward
lifecycle
security classification
retention policy
validation policy

Governance SHALL accompany every governed object.

5. Data Quality Principles

Atlas data SHALL satisfy the following quality attributes.

Attribute	Description
Accuracy	Correct engineering meaning
Completeness	Required information present
Consistency	No conflicting representations
Validity	Conforms to canonical definitions
Timeliness	Reflects intended temporal context
Integrity	Protected against unauthorized modification
Traceability	Fully reconstructable history
Explainability	Engineering rationale preserved
Reusability	Suitable for future cognition
Interoperability	Exchangeable across systems
6. Canonical Object Requirements

Every Atlas object SHALL possess:

globally unique identity
canonical type
metadata
lifecycle state
provenance
traceability
version
governance metadata

These requirements are inherited by all Atlas objects.

7. Relationship Principles

Relationships SHALL be:

explicit
directional
typed
traceable
version-aware

Relationship semantics SHALL be canonical.

8. Lifecycle Principles

Every Atlas object SHALL support:

Created
    │
Validated
    │
Active
    │
Versioned
    │
Archived
    │
Retired

Lifecycle transitions SHALL preserve historical integrity.

9. Validation Principles

Atlas objects SHALL be validated against:

schema
semantics
constraints
governance
relationships
version compatibility

Validation SHALL occur before acceptance into the Atlas ecosystem.

10. Conformance

A canonical Atlas object conforms if it:

preserves semantic integrity.
implements mandatory metadata.
supports versioning.
maintains provenance.
preserves traceability.
complies with governance requirements.
11. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

semantic profiles
ontology mappings
digital twin extensions
engineering domain specializations
federated engineering standards

Such extensions SHALL remain consistent with these principles.